module std.page_fold

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, Int, Bool, List, String }
import std.integer { PositiveInt, NonNegativeInt }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, uri_wire }

// THREE CLAIMS, NOT ONE, AND CONFLATING THEM IS WHY THIS MODULE DID NOT EXIST FOR SO LONG.
//
// A listing read makes three separable claims and every one of them can be true while the others
// are false. (1) PAGE SIZE is how many items one request asks for -- a request parameter, bounded
// by the serving authority. (2) CURSOR SHAPE is how the next request is named -- an offset, an
// opaque token, or a link target, and they are different protocols rather than spellings of one.
// (3) CONTINUATION CLOSURE is whether the sequence of requests was consumed until the authority
// said there was no next -- a property of the WHOLE fold, which no single response carries.
//
// Two sessions read a `per_page` field and concluded pagination was modeled: they substituted
// claim (1) for claim (3). The substitution is easy precisely because claim (1) is the one that
// appears lexically in a request declaration while claim (3) appears nowhere unless a fold
// exists. So the three are declared here as three carriers, and no projection of one answers for
// another.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the effect that fetches a page. This module is the pure driver --
// it decides, given what a page said, whether another request is owed, whether the fold is closed,
// or whether it must refuse. The transport that performs the request is a realization bound at the
// caller's boundary (DESIGN section 3: the dispatch that selects a realization is itself
// realization). The driver is total and executable against a recorded page sequence, which is what
// page_fold_run consumes.

// THE BOUND IS THE POINT, AND IT IS A CONSTRUCTOR RATHER THAN A CHECK.
//
// An external listing has no modeled length. A fold that follows continuations until the authority
// stops is an unbounded external read, and an unbounded external read is the absorbing fallback
// DESIGN section 5 forbids: a serving side that keeps answering `next` -- through a defect, a
// cursor that does not advance, or a corpus larger than anyone budgeted -- turns a read into a
// non-terminating one, and the honest arms are exhaustion or refusal, never a truncated result
// wearing the shape of a complete one.
//
// max_pages is PositiveInt rather than Int because a zero-page budget would refuse every fold
// before it begins -- a policy nobody means, reported with the same refusal as a genuine overrun.
// The single authority for positivity is std.integer, consumed rather than re-minted.
//
// THE REFINEMENT IS A PARTIAL WALL, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
// Probed on this tree: a LITERAL violation refuses (`PageSize { count: 0 }` gives "expected
// 'Product(PositiveInt)', got 'Primitive(Int)'") and a literal `50` is admitted, so the refinement
// is genuinely evaluated for literals. An `Int`-typed DECLARATION REFERENCE is admitted WITHOUT
// that check, whatever its value. `as PositiveInt` is not an escape either: it refuses at
// evaluation, because `Int` is not `Nat` and the conversion is not representable.
//
// So the guarantee this field carries is: no caller can write a non-positive bound as a literal.
// A caller routing a non-positive `Int` row into it is not stopped by this type today. Every
// construction in this repository passes a literal or a positive cited row, so no live value is
// wrong -- the gap is in what the type FORBIDS, not in what it currently holds. Saying "PositiveInt
// therefore it cannot be zero" without that qualifier would be the prose-over-enforcement claim
// this module has already had to correct once.
type PageBound {
  max_pages: PositiveInt
}

// PAGE SIZE, CLAIM ONE, AND ITS ADMISSION AGAINST A SERVING MAXIMUM.
//
// The serving maximum is a fact about the authority being read, so it is supplied by the extdeps
// module that cites it and is never defaulted here. The admission REFUSES an over-large request
// rather than clamping it: an authority that silently clamps (GitHub's REST API is documented to)
// answers a request that was never made, and the caller has no way to learn that the size it
// reasoned about is not the size it got. Refusing keeps the disagreement visible at the boundary
// where it is still cheap.
type PageSize {
  count: PositiveInt
}

type PageSizeAdmission
  = PageSizeAdmitted { size: PageSize }
  | PageSizeAboveAuthorityMaximum { requested: PageSize, maximum: PageSize }

fn admit_page_size(requested: PageSize, maximum: PageSize) -> PageSizeAdmission {
  if requested.count > maximum.count {
    PageSizeAboveAuthorityMaximum { requested: requested, maximum: maximum }
  } else {
    PageSizeAdmitted { size: requested }
  }
}

// CURSOR SHAPE, CLAIM TWO, AS A CLOSED COPRODUCT OF THE FORMS REAL AUTHORITIES DOCUMENT.
//
// It is closed rather than a String because the three forms have different advance laws and
// different failure modes, and a String erases which one is in play. An offset advances by
// arithmetic the CLIENT performs and is therefore the form that can loop forever if the server
// stops honouring it; an opaque token advances only by echoing what the server said and must never
// be interpreted; a link target is a whole request the server authored (RFC 8288 rel="next"), so
// the client composes no request at all.
//
// Adding a fourth form is a row here, and the exhaustive matches below fail to compile until every
// consumer has said what it means -- which is the point of closing it.
type PageCursor
  = OffsetPage { page: PositiveInt }
  | OpaqueToken { token: NonEmptyStr }
  | LinkNext { target: Uri }

fn page_cursor_shape_key(c: PageCursor) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    OffsetPage { page: p } => "offset_page" as NonEmptyStr
    OpaqueToken { token: t } => "opaque_token" as NonEmptyStr
    LinkNext { target: u } => "link_next" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// EQUALITY IS NOT UNIFORM ACROSS THE ARMS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS FUNCTION EXISTS.
//
// `==` already decides a closed coproduct natively -- true for like variants, false for unlike,
// payload-comparing on payload-carrying arms -- so re-deriving it arm by arm would be a second
// representation of a native relation, the `parallel_representation_debt` class. Two of the three
// arms are therefore left to `==` rather than restated.
//
// LinkNext is the exception and it is a SEMANTIC one, not a stylistic preference. A cursor named
// by a link target is the same cursor when it addresses the same resource, and two `Uri` values
// that differ in internal representation may address the same resource. Structural equality would
// call those two distinct and the fold would follow the same page forever, reporting a budget
// overrun for a cycle. So this arm compares WIRE FORM, which is the identity the serving authority
// actually keys on, and the arm-by-arm shape is what makes that single deviation visible.
fn page_cursor_eq(a: PageCursor, b: PageCursor) -> Bool {
  match a {
    LinkNext { target: ua } => match b {
      LinkNext { target: ub } => uri_wire(uri: ua) == uri_wire(uri: ub)
      OffsetPage { page: pb } => false
      OpaqueToken { token: tb } => false
    }
    OffsetPage { page: pa } => a == b
    OpaqueToken { token: ta } => a == b
  }
}

// CONTINUATION CLOSURE, CLAIM THREE. What ONE page says about whether another is owed.
//
// ContinuationClosed is the authority's statement that the sequence ends here. It is not the same
// fact as "this page was short", and the difference is why a short-page heuristic is not offered:
// a page shorter than the requested size is evidence about the serving side's batching, not about
// exhaustion, and reading it as exhaustion silently truncates exactly the listings whose last full
// page happens to land on a boundary.
type PageContinuation
  = ContinuationClosed
  | ContinuationNext { cursor: PageCursor }

// THE SUBJECT IS WHAT MAKES A REFUSAL LOCATED. A refusal that says only "the page budget ran out"
// names no operation, so a consumer holding several folds cannot tell which listing is incomplete.
type PageFoldSubject {
  operation: NonEmptyStr
}

// EVERY REFUSAL ARM CARRIES ITS SUBJECT AND ITS POSITION, and none of them carries a partial
// result. That absence is the wall: DESIGN section 5 requires the failure arm to refuse rather
// than widen, and a refusal that also handed back the items read so far would let a caller use a
// truncated listing by reading one field and ignoring the other.
//
// The arms are separate because their remedies are separate. A budget overrun is a policy question
// -- raise the bound, or narrow the query. A non-advancing cursor is a defect on the serving side
// or in the cursor projection, and folding it into the budget arm would report a real infinite
// loop as a budget that was set too low, sending the operator to change the wrong number. A shape
// change mid-fold means two protocols answered one listing. A fold still open when the page
// sequence ran out is the caller's own truncation, and it is the arm that catches a transport
// that stopped early without saying so.
type PageFoldRefusal
  = PageBudgetExhausted {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      max_pages: PositiveInt
      pages_read: NonNegativeInt
      stopped_at: PageCursor
    }
  | CursorDidNotAdvance {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      page_index: Int
      cursor: PageCursor
    }
  | ContinuationShapeChanged {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      page_index: Int
      from_shape: NonEmptyStr
      to_shape: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | ContinuationNotObserved {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      pages_read: NonNegativeInt
      awaiting: PageCursor
    }
  | PageObservedAfterFoldEnded {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      pages_read: NonNegativeInt
    }

fn page_fold_refusal_subject(r: PageFoldRefusal) -> PageFoldSubject {
  match r {
    PageBudgetExhausted { subject: s, max_pages: m, pages_read: n, stopped_at: c } => s
    CursorDidNotAdvance { subject: s, page_index: i, cursor: c } => s
    ContinuationShapeChanged { subject: s, page_index: i, from_shape: f, to_shape: t } => s
    ContinuationNotObserved { subject: s, pages_read: n, awaiting: c } => s
    PageObservedAfterFoldEnded { subject: s, pages_read: n } => s
  }
}

fn page_fold_refusal_key(r: PageFoldRefusal) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r {
    PageBudgetExhausted { subject: s, max_pages: m, pages_read: n, stopped_at: c } => "page_budget_exhausted" as NonEmptyStr
    CursorDidNotAdvance { subject: s, page_index: i, cursor: c } => "cursor_did_not_advance" as NonEmptyStr
    ContinuationShapeChanged { subject: s, page_index: i, from_shape: f, to_shape: t } => "continuation_shape_changed" as NonEmptyStr
    ContinuationNotObserved { subject: s, pages_read: n, awaiting: c } => "continuation_not_observed" as NonEmptyStr
    PageObservedAfterFoldEnded { subject: s, pages_read: n } => "page_observed_after_fold_ended" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// ONE PAGE AS THE DRIVER SEES IT: the items it carried, and what it said about a next one.
type PageObservation<I> {
  items: List<I>
  continuation: PageContinuation
}

type PageFoldStatus
  = FoldOpen { next: PageCursor }
  | FoldEnded
  | FoldRefused { refusal: PageFoldRefusal }

// THE SHAPE THE SERVING SIDE HAS SPOKEN SO FAR, WHICH IS NOT THE SHAPE THE CALLER OPENED WITH.
//
// This distinction was found by binding the fold to a real operation and is worth stating rather
// than merely fixing. `extdeps.github.workflow_runs` ListForRef is ADDRESSED by query parameters
// and CONTINUES by an RFC 8288 Link header, so its first cursor is an offset and every
// continuation after it is a link target. Comparing each continuation against the CURRENT cursor
// made the first page of every such listing look like a protocol switch, and the fold refused on
// page one -- a wall that fires on the correct end state, which DESIGN's own repair-trap note says
// is a guard ratifying a coincidence rather than replacing it.
//
// The invariant that is actually worth holding is that the SERVING SIDE does not switch protocols
// mid-stream. How the caller addressed page one is a different fact and is allowed to differ. So
// the shape is unset until the authority has spoken once, and is compared only against what the
// authority said before.
type ContinuationShapeSeen
  = ShapeUnset
  | ShapeSeen { key: NonEmptyStr }

// THE STATE HAS NO CONSTRUCTOR OUTSIDE THIS MODULE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE OUTCOME CLAIM TRUE.
//
// `PageFoldOutcome` carrying items only on the closed arm is worth nothing if a caller can hand
// `page_fold_finish` a state that SAYS it closed. As a plain record this type admitted exactly
// that: `status: FoldEnded` beside forged items, zero pages read and an empty demand history is a
// perfectly well-typed value, and `page_fold_finish` would have turned it into `PagesClosed` --
// so the guarantee rested on callers choosing to use `page_fold_begin` and `page_fold_observe`
// rather than on anything the compiler enforces. That is validation wearing a constructor's
// clothes, and reporting it as structural was rung inflation on this module's central claim.
//
// `sole_constructor` refuses construction outside `std.page_fold`, so the only way to obtain a
// state at all is `page_fold_begin`, and the only way to move one is `page_fold_observe`. Those
// two are the entire writer set, and both maintain the invariant, so `FoldEnded` is reachable
// only from an observation that carried `ContinuationClosed` -- the authority's own statement that
// the listing ended.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. The arms still carry fields that are meaningless on some of them (a
// demand history on an ended fold), so contradictory FIELD COMBINATIONS remain writable INSIDE
// this module even though no state at all is writable outside it. Splitting the status into
// coproduct arms that carry only their own valid fields would close that too; it is a further
// climb against this module's two internal writers, not the boundary that consumers stand behind.
//
// THE DISCRIMINATING RED IS A RESOLVE REFUSAL, AND IT WAS EXECUTED RATHER THAN ARGUED. A probe
// forging `status: FoldEnded` beside items from the witness module -- the exact state finding 1
// named -- was authored and run, and the compiler refused it, located:
//
//   page_fold_witness_test.dag:482:16: error: sole_constructor type 'PageFoldState'
//   cannot be constructed outside its defining module
//
// The probe is AUTHORABLE and was run; what it cannot be is ENROLLED, because a module carrying it
// does not resolve, so it has no passing row to contribute. That distinction is the honest one: the
// wall is proven to fire by execution, and the reason no row appears in the suite is the refusal's
// own strength rather than an unbuilt harness. The probe is not committed -- an unresolvable module
// would red the corpus -- so this comment is where the receipt lives.
type PageFoldState<I> sole_constructor {
  subject: PageFoldSubject
  bound: PageBound
  pages_read: NonNegativeInt
  items: List<I>
  shape: ContinuationShapeSeen
  demanded: List<PageCursor>
  status: PageFoldStatus
}

fn page_fold_begin<I>(subject: PageFoldSubject, bound: PageBound, first: PageCursor) -> PageFoldState<I> {
  PageFoldState {
    subject: subject
    bound: bound
    pages_read: 0
    items: []
    shape: ShapeUnset
    demanded: [first]
    status: FoldOpen { next: first }
  }
}

// WHAT THE DRIVER OWES BEFORE IT MAY OBSERVE ANOTHER PAGE. A caller drives the transport from this
// projection rather than from the state's fields, so "is another request owed" is answered in one
// place instead of once per transport.
type PageFoldDemand
  = DemandPage { cursor: PageCursor }
  | DemandNone

fn page_fold_demand<I>(state: PageFoldState<I>) -> PageFoldDemand {
  match state.status {
    FoldOpen { next: c } => DemandPage { cursor: c }
    FoldEnded => DemandNone
    FoldRefused { refusal: r } => DemandNone
  }
}

type ContinuationShapeAdmission
  = ShapeAdmitted
  | ShapeRefused { from_shape: NonEmptyStr }

fn continuation_shape_admission(seen: ContinuationShapeSeen, offered: NonEmptyStr) -> ContinuationShapeAdmission {
  match seen {
    ShapeUnset => ShapeAdmitted
    ShapeSeen { key: k } => if k == offered { ShapeAdmitted } else { ShapeRefused { from_shape: k } }
  }
}

// THE ONE TRANSITION. Every arm below either ends the fold, refuses, or advances to a cursor NOT
// ALREADY DEMANDED, and the page count rises by exactly one per observation -- the descent that
// makes the bound a real bound rather than a hope.
//
// THE NON-ADVANCE CHECK IS MEMBERSHIP IN EVERY CURSOR DEMANDED SO FAR, NOT A COMPARISON WITH THE
// CURRENT ONE, and the difference is a defect this module shipped with. Comparing only the current
// cursor catches page 1 -> page 1 and misses page 1 -> page 2 -> page 1: every individual step
// advances, so the cycle runs until the budget stops it and reports `PageBudgetExhausted`. That is
// the wrong diagnosis for a cycling authority and it is a §5 widen -- the fold terminated, so
// nothing was unsafe, but the refusal named a budget that was adequate rather than the cursor
// sequence that never converged, and those have different remedies. Immediate non-advancement is
// the length-one case of revisiting, so ONE arm covers both rather than two arms splitting a
// single class.
//
// The comparison is O(pages) per observation and so O(max_pages^2) per fold. That is bounded by a
// declared constructor rather than by the corpus, which is the property that makes it payable:
// max_pages is small and authored, and the alternative is a cheaper check that answers wrongly.
//
// THE BOUND IS CHECKED WHEN A FURTHER PAGE IS DEMANDED, not when one is delivered, and the
// distinction is load-bearing. A listing that fits in exactly max_pages pages and then says
// ContinuationClosed must CLOSE, not refuse: checking on delivery would refuse the last page of
// every listing whose length happens to equal the budget, which is a false incompleteness report
// about a listing that was in fact read to exhaustion.
fn page_fold_observe<I>(state: PageFoldState<I>, page: PageObservation<I>) -> PageFoldState<I> {
  match state.status {
    FoldEnded => PageFoldState {
      subject: state.subject
      bound: state.bound
      pages_read: state.pages_read
      items: state.items
      shape: state.shape
      demanded: state.demanded
      status: FoldRefused {
        refusal: PageObservedAfterFoldEnded { subject: state.subject, pages_read: state.pages_read }
      }
    }
    FoldRefused { refusal: r } => state
    FoldOpen { next: _ } =>
      let read = state.pages_read + 1
      let carried = concat(state.items, page.items)
      match page.continuation {
        ContinuationClosed => PageFoldState {
          subject: state.subject
          bound: state.bound
          pages_read: read
          items: carried
          shape: state.shape
          demanded: state.demanded
          status: FoldEnded
        }
        ContinuationNext { cursor: nxt } =>
          let next_shape = page_cursor_shape_key(c: nxt)
          if (state.demanded |> any(d => page_cursor_eq(a: d, b: nxt))) {
            PageFoldState {
              subject: state.subject
              bound: state.bound
              pages_read: read
              items: state.items
              shape: state.shape
              demanded: state.demanded
              status: FoldRefused {
                refusal: CursorDidNotAdvance { subject: state.subject, page_index: read, cursor: nxt }
              }
            }
          } else {
            match continuation_shape_admission(seen: state.shape, offered: next_shape) {
              ShapeRefused { from_shape: from_key } => PageFoldState {
                subject: state.subject
                bound: state.bound
                pages_read: read
                items: state.items
                shape: state.shape
                demanded: state.demanded
                status: FoldRefused {
                  refusal: ContinuationShapeChanged {
                    subject: state.subject
                    page_index: read
                    from_shape: from_key
                    to_shape: next_shape
                  }
                }
              }
              ShapeAdmitted =>
                if read >= state.bound.max_pages {
                  PageFoldState {
                    subject: state.subject
                    bound: state.bound
                    pages_read: read
                    items: state.items
                    shape: ShapeSeen { key: next_shape }
                    demanded: state.demanded
                    status: FoldRefused {
                      refusal: PageBudgetExhausted {
                        subject: state.subject
                        max_pages: state.bound.max_pages
                        pages_read: read
                        stopped_at: nxt
                      }
                    }
                  }
                } else {
                  PageFoldState {
                    subject: state.subject
                    bound: state.bound
                    pages_read: read
                    items: carried
                    shape: ShapeSeen { key: next_shape }
                    demanded: concat(state.demanded, [nxt])
                    status: FoldOpen { next: nxt }
                  }
                }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE OUTCOME CARRIES THE ITEMS ONLY ON THE CLOSED ARM. This is the module's safety claim stated
// as a constructor: a consumer cannot hold a list of items from a fold that did not reach the
// authority's own end-of-listing, let alone mistake one for a complete listing.
//
// THE CLAIM IS LOAD-BEARING ONLY BECAUSE `PageFoldState` IS `sole_constructor`. This arm is
// reachable from `page_fold_finish`, which reads a state -- so without a construction wall on the
// state the sentence above would be false, satisfiable by forging a closed-looking state rather
// than by reading a closed listing. The two facts are one guarantee and neither half stands alone.
type PageFoldOutcome<I>
  = PagesClosed {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      pages_read: NonNegativeInt
      items: List<I>
    }
  | PagesRefused { refusal: PageFoldRefusal }

fn page_fold_finish<I>(state: PageFoldState<I>) -> PageFoldOutcome<I> {
  match state.status {
    FoldEnded => PagesClosed {
      subject: state.subject
      pages_read: state.pages_read
      items: state.items
    }
    FoldRefused { refusal: r } => PagesRefused { refusal: r }
    FoldOpen { next: c } => PagesRefused {
      refusal: ContinuationNotObserved {
        subject: state.subject
        pages_read: state.pages_read
        awaiting: c
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE DRIVER OVER A RECORDED PAGE SEQUENCE. It is a fold over the pages a transport actually
// delivered, so it executes -- under the interpreter, over a controlled fixture, or over a
// replayed live read -- rather than describing what an executing fold would do.
//
// A live transport calls page_fold_demand / page_fold_observe in its own loop instead; this
// projection exists because the two must not be two state machines, and the recorded form is the
// one a witness can drive.
fn page_fold_run<I>(
  subject: PageFoldSubject,
  bound: PageBound,
  first: PageCursor,
  pages: List<PageObservation<I>>
) -> PageFoldOutcome<I> {
  page_fold_finish(
    state: fold_list(
      xs: pages,
      empty: page_fold_begin(subject: subject, bound: bound, first: first),
      cons: fn(st, p) { page_fold_observe(state: st, page: p) }
    )
  )
}

// THE LIVE DRIVER. `page_fold_run` folds a page sequence someone already has; this one PERFORMS
// the reads, calling `fetch` for each cursor the fold demands until the authority closes the
// listing or a refusal fires.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS MODULE DECLARED THAT THIS COULD NOT BE WRITTEN, and the correction is
// recorded here rather than quietly dropped, because the false claim was the more dangerous
// artifact. It said the substrate had no construct for an effectful loop whose continuation is
// decided by the value the previous effect returned, and it named that construct as a capability
// trigger. THAT WAS ASSERTED, NOT MEASURED, AND IT IS FALSE. Recursion is sugar over Loop and a
// `.dag` function may invoke a service operation, so a recursive function whose next argument is
// derived from the previous call's effect result IS the construct. MEASURED, not reasoned: a probe
// descending a directory tree, where each iteration's path came from the previous listing's
// entries, executed three chained effects -- and it was run only after a control that MUST fail
// actually failed, because two earlier readings of the same probe came back clean while the
// compiler was never reaching the module at all.
//
// A trigger naming an absence that does not exist is permanent by construction: nothing can retire
// it, and it would have been cited as the reason no live pager was built. So it is deleted rather
// than softened, and this driver stands in its place.
//
// `fetch` IS THE EFFECT AND IT IS THE CALLER'S. This module performs no read of its own and names
// no transport: which operation is invoked, over which protocol, under which credential, is a
// realization fact that belongs at the caller's boundary (DESIGN section 3 -- the dispatch that
// selects a realization is itself realization). What lives here is the bound, the advance and the
// closure, which are the same whatever the transport.
//
// TERMINATION IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN TRUSTED. Every recursive call passes through
// `page_fold_observe`, which either ends the fold, refuses, or advances to a cursor that is not the
// one just demanded, and which increments the page count -- so the descent is on the budget and the
// recursion is bounded by `bound.max_pages` no matter what the serving side answers.
fn page_fold_live<I>(
  subject: PageFoldSubject,
  bound: PageBound,
  first: PageCursor,
  fetch: fn(PageCursor) -> PageObservation<I>
) -> PageFoldOutcome<I> {
  page_fold_pump(
    state: page_fold_begin(subject: subject, bound: bound, first: first),
    fetch: fetch
  )
}

fn page_fold_pump<I>(
  state: PageFoldState<I>,
  fetch: fn(PageCursor) -> PageObservation<I>
) -> PageFoldOutcome<I> {
  match page_fold_demand(state: state) {
    DemandNone => page_fold_finish(state: state)
    DemandPage { cursor: c } =>
      page_fold_pump(state: page_fold_observe(state: state, page: fetch(c)), fetch: fetch)
  }
}
